    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "The Sasanian Empire emerged under Ardashir I in 224 CE and became a major power across Iran, Mesopotamia, and neighboring regions. Its royal culture, religious institutions, and exchanges with Rome and Byzantium shaped late antiquity and continued to influence the early Islamic world. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Sasanians", "Late antiquity", "Ardashir I", "Ctesiphon", "Zoroastrianism", "Byzantium"],
    "status": "Historical empire; last Sasanian emperor died in 651 CE",
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T12:01:10Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T12:01:10Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Sasanian Empire: Kingship, Religion and Late Antiquity | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Sasanian government, religious diversity, rivalry with Byzantium, royal imagery, and continuities after the empire's fall."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "imperial-geography",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An Iranian dynasty with a Mesopotamian center",
        "content": "In 224 CE, Ardashir I overthrew Parthian power and founded the Sasanian dynasty. His regional base was Fars in southern Iran, but the empire's political geography extended well beyond the Iranian plateau. Ctesiphon, near modern Baghdad in Iraq, was a major royal center, alongside cities such as Firuzabad and Bishapur. The Smithsonian describes an empire consolidated through a common bureaucratic framework and multiple regional capitals. [s1]\n\nIts borders fluctuated. Egypt and parts of the eastern Mediterranean came under Sasanian control during periods of expansion; they were not continuously held provinces throughout the dynasty's history. A map of maximum extent therefore answers a different question from a map of enduring imperial centers. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "royal-images",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Making kingship visible",
        "content": "Sasanian rulers placed their achievements in landscapes already associated with earlier monarchy. At Naqsh-e Rustam, they carved reliefs below the Achaemenid royal tombs. The juxtaposition made a claim about continuity and legitimacy; it does not by itself demonstrate detailed knowledge of every earlier king. The Smithsonian also interprets triumphal reliefs as political messaging directed against Rome. [s1]\n\nCoins carried royal portraits and fire altars, connecting the ruler's public image with religious authority. Their repeated imagery brought this association into a very different setting from the monumental cliff face. Both forms help explain what the monarchy wished to communicate, while leaving questions about reception among different communities open. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "religion-society",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Religious patronage and unequal belonging",
        "content": "Zoroastrianism received royal support, but Sasanian society also included substantial Jewish and Christian communities. Religious coexistence cannot be reduced to a single empire-wide policy of tolerance. The legal historian Mansour Shaki's account in Encyclopaedia Iranica emphasizes that a person's position depended on social class, sex, and religious and ethnic affiliation. These were unequal forms of belonging, unlike a modern ideal of uniform citizenship. [s3]\n\nRelations with minority communities changed between reigns. Non-Zoroastrians could attain positions at court, and communities could practice their religions, yet harassment and persecution also occurred. Shaki identifies the persecution of Christians under Shapur II as a particularly severe episode. Evidence of intellectual flourishing or royal employment must therefore be read alongside evidence of coercion. Neither establishes a timeless condition for every region and community. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "exchange-and-conflict",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Rivalry also produced exchange",
        "content": "Rome and its eastern continuation, conventionally called the Byzantine Empire, were military competitors and cultural interlocutors. Frontier towns and the circulation of people and objects connected their worlds. Sasanian textiles, silverware, and decorative motifs attracted interest beyond the empire's political boundaries. Their adoption in Byzantine settings shows that admiration and hostility could coexist. [s2]\n\nWar nevertheless had material consequences. The Metropolitan Museum discusses a letter on a pottery fragment from Persian-occupied Egypt that concerns grain supply. Such an object connects imperial campaigns with provisioning rather than treating conflict only as a succession of royal victories. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "chronology",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Selected developments and the transition beyond Sasanian rule",
        "content": "```mermaid\ntimeline\n  title Selected Sasanian developments\n  224 CE : Ardashir I establishes Sasanian rule\n  Third century CE : Shapur I expands imperial power\n  614 CE : Sasanian forces capture Jerusalem\n  651 CE : Yazdegerd III dies\n  Later seventh century CE : Sasanian-style coinage continues under Islamic rule\n```",
        "summary": "These selected milestones distinguish the foundation and expansion of the dynasty from its political end and the later survival of artistic and monetary forms. The sequence does not imply that all territories changed rulers at once.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "afterlife",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A political ending with cultural continuities",
        "content": "Yazdegerd III, the last Sasanian emperor, died in 651 CE amid the Arab conquests. The end of the dynasty did not erase its material culture. Early Islamic rulers continued or adapted techniques and motifs associated with Sasanian silks, silver vessels, architectural decoration, and coinage. These continuities make the empire essential to understanding the early Islamic world as well as pre-Islamic Iran. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
